  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of Displacement-Related Data and Health &amp; Protection Assistance for Mobile Populations in Democratic Republic of the Congo</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since March 2022, North Kivu province of the Democratic Republic of the Congo has experienced insecurity due to the resumption of conflict between the Mouvement du 23 mars (M23) and the Forces Arm&#xe9;es de la R&#xe9;publique D&#xe9;mocratique du Congo (FARDC) and their allies, resulting in a severe humanitarian crisis. Since the resurgence of the M23 group, the number of displaced people has increased significantly, with 590,786 individuals displaced though 818,266 others have reportedly returned to their area of origin, as of August 2023. While the needs of populations in displacement persist, there is also a growing need in parallel to support communities who have returned.Through this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) contributes to strengthening the humanitarian response in areas of displacement and return, with a focus on preparing durable solutions and enhancing health systems. IOM is carrying out a return index assessment in Rutshuru territory to inform the broader response of the humanitarian and development community; distributing flashlights and disseminate gender-based violence risk mitigation messages; rolling out the Camp Coordination and Camp Management Cluster strategy in North Kivu province; and reinforcing the capacity of the National Border Hygiene Program to deliver healthcare services.</narrative>
  </description>
